I wish I could remember where I got it from; it's not an uncommon
position in the circles of people I know who discuss it.  Mutter.

Tolkien and C. S. Lewis did a lot of writing back and forth, and
certainly informed each other's positions and thoughts on their writing.
Both were fairly heavily influenced by their faith.

As I understand it, _The Lord of the Rings_ and associated stuff was
Tolkien's attempt to create a mythology for England, in part; the world
is intended to be a mythic age of our own, one that contains the same
truths.  For Tolkien, writing a story that has very strong moral
undertones, those "same truths" are going to be the truths later
revealed differently by Christianity, which is in the future of his
world.

